🔍 Description
This project presents a chatbot designed to classify user intents using a fine-tuned DistilBERT model on the CLINC150 "out-of-scope" dataset. The system is capable of understanding natural language input (text or voice), classifying it into one of 150 in-scope categories—or detecting it as out-of-scope—based on contextual understanding using transformer-based models.

Developed with a focus on combining modern NLP models with a user-friendly interface, the chatbot is deployed through a Streamlit application and integrates voice input using the SpeechRecognition library.

🚀 Features
✅ Multi-class intent classification (150+1 classes)

✅ Fine-tuned distilbert-base-uncased model

✅ Voice input using microphone (SpeechRecognition)

✅ Web interface using Streamlit

✅ ~89% accuracy on held-out test set

✅ Handles both typed and spoken queries

🗂 Dataset
Source: clinc_oos from Hugging Face datasets

Structure:

text: The user query

intent: Intent category

Why this dataset? It includes 150 real-world intents + 1 out-of-scope label, making it ideal for testing classification boundaries.

🧠 Model Details
Model: DistilBERT (distilbert-base-uncased)

Framework: Hugging Face Transformers

Training API: Trainer class

Metrics: Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score

Final Accuracy: ~89%

🧪 Preprocessing
Tokenization using DistilBertTokenizerFast

No need for stemming or lemmatization due to tokenizer design

Lowercasing applied

🖥️ User Interface
Built with Streamlit

Supports voice queries via microphone input

Interactive display of predicted intent

📈 Evaluation
Tested on held-out test set

Manual tests on edge cases

Confusion matrix used for analysis

🌱 Future Work
Add fallback rules for uncertain predictions

Explore BERT/RoBERTa for higher accuracy

Integrate response generation (not just intent detection)

Deploy on Streamlit Cloud or Hugging Face Spaces

